  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Truist grows revenue ~4% (guide 3.5-4% for FY2026) as fee income compounds and NII grows slowly through the deliberate exit from auto/marine/RV lending. Expense discipline adds ~1.6pp of pre-tax margin over five years; ROTCE settles around 15%, below the 16-18% long-term target. Credit stays near the guided ~55bps NCO. Excess CET1 above the 10% target is returned, half of it credited.",
    "bull": "The new CEO's commercial/payments strategy works: fee businesses keep growing ~10%, commercial loan growth accelerates in the Southeast, and the efficiency ratio improves ~4pp, delivering ROTCE in the 17-18% range and ~5% revenue growth for three years.",
    "bear": "A regional downturn hits in year 2: CRE (14% office) and consumer credit push charge-offs to ~110bps, revenue shrinks 1% as rate cuts and the auto run-off compress NII, and buybacks pause to rebuild capital. Recovery is to a structurally lower ~13% ROTCE."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Truist is valued as a bank: free cash flow to equity is earnings after the capital retention needed to keep CET1 flat as the balance sheet grows, discounted at the cost of equity, with preferred deducted and deposits treated as operating funding (net debt zero). The question is whether a ~15% ROTCE franchise at ~1.4x tangible book is cheap; at a 10.5% cost of equity a 15% ROTCE growing 3-4% justifies roughly 1.6-1.7x tangible book.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Credit cycle: CRE ($28.9B, 14% office) and consumer losses; each +10bps of NCO on ~$320B loans is ~$320M pre-tax (~$0.22/share of annual earnings)",
      "Rate path / NIM: NII guided only +1-1.5% for FY2026 as the auto/marine run-off trades yield for quality",
      "Capital rules: Basel III endgame or AOCI recognition could remove the excess-capital credit and slow buybacks",
      "Execution under new CEO Michael Lyons (from Sept 2026); the 16-18% ROTCE target is not assumed in base",
      "Tax: the ~14.5% effective rate relies on tax-credit investments; base ramps to 17%"
    ]
  },
